# ======================================================================
# library.agg  --  the shipped DecL library
# ======================================================================
#
# Version: 1
#
# PURPOSE
# -------
# ONE library. It replaces examples.agg (the old curated set),
# cookbook.agg (the feature walkthrough) and actuarial-severity-curves.agg
# (the severity reference), which were three files with overlapping
# purpose, three different naming conventions, and no machine-readable
# grouping. It is the default `build` recipe base, the source the Quarto
# cookbook renders from, and the source the aggregate_api (aLL) SPA reads
# for its Examples dropdown.
#
# NAMES are descriptive and GLOBALLY UNIQUE across kinds, so `build('X')`
# and `build.recipe('X')` always mean the same entry and no `kind=`
# disambiguator is ever needed. The old single-letter filing prefixes
# (A., B., K. ...) are gone -- grouping is what tags{} is for. Names that
# lead with a citekey (Mack2003.Lognorm) keep it: that is a citation, not
# a filing code.
#
# THE TRAILER carries everything a tool reads. Comments are for humans
# only -- nothing in src/aggregate parses them (the lexer deletes them
# before the grammar ever sees a line).
#
#   note{...}       one-line description. The SPA dropdown blurb.
#   tags{...}       comma- and/or space-separated slugs; grouping and
#                   selection (build.discover(tags=...)).
#   hints{...}      key=value; build settings, caller-wins.
#   doc{{{...}}}    the long-form recipe: Problem / Solution / Discussion
#                   / Check, markdown with fenced python. Rendered by the
#                   cookbook and RUN by tests/test_library_recipes.py, so
#                   an entry documents and tests itself. Closing fence
#                   must be alone on its line.
#
# WRITING A doc: two conventions that matter.
#
#   <<decl>>   NEVER retype the program in the Solution. Write
#
#                  ```python
#                  a = build('''<<decl>>''')
#                  ```
#
#              and the entry's own declaration is substituted in, rendered
#              canonically from the spec. Two copies of one program inside a
#              single statement would drift the first time either was edited;
#              removing that trap is the whole point.
#
#              The substitution carries `hints{...}` and NOTHING else, because
#              hints change how the object BUILDS: a copy-pasteable program
#              without them would not reproduce the recipe. The note is the
#              recipe's Problem, the tags are the page it sits on, and the doc
#              is the page itself, so repeating any of them inside the program
#              would be redundant. Dropping the doc is also the recursion
#              guard: a doc must not quote itself.
#
#              Being canonical, the substituted form can differ cosmetically
#              from what is typed above (e.g. `50 po 200 xs A` renders
#              `25% po 200 xs A`). That is normalization, not a change.
#
#   ```python  Plain fences, NOT ```{python}. A doc body is DATA: it is stored
#              in the .agg file, executed by aggregate.recipe.Recipe.run (the
#              pytest harness), and rendered to a book by aggregate.cookbook,
#              which rewrites each plain fence into a ```{python} Quarto cell
#              on the way out. Writing ```{python} here would put Quarto
#              source syntax into data and render as a literal label.
#
#              The code inside the fence is passed through untouched, which is
#              what makes the page and the test run byte-identical programs.
#              So a fence may open with a Quarto cell option:
#
#                  ```python
#                  #| fig-cap: "The distortion, above the diagonal."
#                  g.plot()
#                  ```
#
#              Quarto honors it; Recipe.run sees a comment. Plain fences also
#              keep the block copy-pasteable and syntax-highlighted anywhere
#              markdown is read. A fence tagged anything else (```text) is
#              prose and is NOT executed: use it to show output.
#
# TAG VOCABULARY -- namespaced, and it never restates the object's TYPE.
#
# Type is what `kind` is for: build.discover(kind='sev') finds every severity
# object. A tag saying `severity` on a `sev` entry would be dead weight, and
# would invite "why does type appear twice, which do I use?". So every tag
# carries a namespace and answers a question `kind` cannot:
#
#   topic:X   what the recipe is ABOUT -- severity frequency aggregate
#             reinsurance pnl portfolio distortion bivariate bounds ruin
#             numerics. Independent of type: an `agg` entry demonstrating a
#             severity form is topic:severity, and that is not a restatement.
#   role:X    where it stands -- hero (landing page) intro (teaching order)
#             reference (a lookup table entry) paper (reproduces a published
#             result).
#   check:X   which invariant the entry is worth checking against. Six
#             archetypes, and the slug is the label without the [Check-]:
#
#               reconciliation      things add up: net + ceded = gross;
#                                   walks foot down the sheet; allocations
#                                   sum to the total.
#               scaling-sweep       vary one knob, watch the output move as
#                                   predicted (sweep a share 25/50/75/100%
#                                   and see ceded premium trace a line).
#               independent-oracle  est_* (FFT) against theoretic moments, a
#                                   closed form, or a hand convolution.
#               limiting-case       sanity bounds: TVaR >= VaR; premium >=
#                                   expected loss; a distortion has g(0)=0,
#                                   g(1)=1 and is concave; zero placement
#                                   gives back gross.
#               round-trip          format_program(build(x)) round-trips;
#                                   two equivalent spellings agree.
#               cross-object        an aggregate priced alone equals its
#                                   allocation in a one-unit portfolio;
#                                   bivariate marginals recover the
#                                   univariates.
#
#             tests/test_library_entries.py is where the checks live.
#   slow      the ONE bare tag, deliberately: it names a pytest marker
#             (tests/test_library_recipes.py maps it to @pytest.mark.slow),
#             not a property of the subject.
#
# Enforced by tests/test_agg_libraries.py -- a bare, un-namespaced tag fails.
#
#   build.discover(kind='sev')                        # by type
#   build.discover(tags='topic:severity')             # by subject
#   build.discover(kind='agg', tags='topic:severity') # both
#
# PLACEMENT: on a `port`, the trailer goes on the `port` header line --
# a trailer written after the last unit binds to that unit, because the
# portfolio's trailer slot closes before the units are read.
#
# HOW MUCH DOCUMENTATION: every entry carries a note{...} -- that is the
# norm, and it is all `discover` and the object dropdown need. A
# doc{{{...}}} is for the cookbook-worthy few: the entries the cookbook
# actually teaches from, where a Problem / Solution / Discussion / Check
# write-up earns its keep. So build.recipes.query('not doc') is a
# DIRECTORY of the library, not a backlog.
#
# LAYOUT: the canonical `spread` form, one clause per line at a two-space
# indent, each statement closed by `;` and separated by a blank line. It is
# what `build.recipe('X').decl` prints and what every generated cookbook page
# shows, so the file and the docs read alike. Regenerate it with
# `python dev/done/reflow_library.py`, which renders each entry through
# `decl_writer.format_program(..., layout='spread')` and refuses to write
# unless every statement still parses to the same spec.
#
# Thirteen entries are exempt, and stay hand-written. The unparser cannot
# invert a named object reference (`sev.UnitSeverity` is resolved and inlined at
# parse time, and since a216 an engine reference like `less agg.USXOLTower`
# writes its whole body out), or the `minimum` distortion combinator (the child
# names are not retained). Three more keep the compact `ssev <c> - <dist>`
# spelling, whose canonical form `-1 * <dist> + <c>` parses two ways. All are
# laid out in the same style by hand; tests/test_agg_libraries.py holds the
# list and checks the rest.
#
# A `sev agg.NAME` reference is NOT in that set: since a293 the writer renders
# it, because the spec records the reference instead of inlining the object.
#
# SECTION ORDER runs starter examples, distortions, severity, frequency,
# aggregates, aggregates with reinsurance, an aggregate as a severity,
# bivariate, profit and loss, numerics, portfolios, papers. It is teaching
# order, and in three places it is also load bearing, because a builtin
# reference resolves by SEQUENTIAL LOAD and the target has to be declared
# first:
#
#   sev.UnitSeverity      leads the severity section, ahead of the frequency
#                         and aggregate entries that use it
#   dist.PHDistortion     and dist.DualDistortion lead the distortion section,
#                         ahead of MinimumDistortion
#   agg.USXOLTower        sits in the reinsurance section, ahead of the xpnl
#                         USHurr that wraps it, and each inner of the
#                         aggregate-as-severity section precedes its outer
#
# Everything else is grouping, and grouping is what tags{} is really for: the
# sections make the FILE readable, `discover` is how a program finds things.
#
# DecL is whitespace-insensitive, so a statement may be laid out however reads
# best. Only two things separate statements: a blank line, or a `;` at the end
# of a line. A comment never does.
# ======================================================================

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Starter examples
# Dice, coins and the plain compound distributions. Everything here is
# exact or nearly so, which is what makes it the place to start.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

agg DiceOneDie
  dfreq [1]
  dsev [1 2 3 4 5 6]
  note{The roll of a single dice as severity.}
  tags{topic:aggregate, role:intro};

agg DiceTwoDice
  dfreq [2]
  dsev [1 2 3 4 5 6]
  note{Sum of the rolls of two dice.}
  tags{topic:aggregate};

agg ThreeDice
  dfreq [3]
  dsev [1 2 3 4 5 6]
  note{Sum of three dice -- exact discrete aggregate; zero validation error.}
  tags{topic:aggregate, role:intro, check:independent-oracle}
  doc{{{
## Problem

You want to see what `aggregate` computes on something you can check in your head. Three dice: a fixed frequency of 3, a severity uniform on 1..6.

## Solution

```python
a = build('''<<decl>>''')
qd(a.summary_df)
```

## Discussion

Everything here is discrete and lands exactly on the grid, so this is the one case where the FFT is not an approximation at all -- the aggregate PMF is the exact three-fold convolution of the die. That is why it is the first recipe: it separates "is the method right" from "is the discretization fine", which every later recipe has to hold apart.

`dfreq [3]` is an explicit outcome list with a single outcome, so N = 3 with probability one. `dsev [1:6]` is the inclusive range 1, 2, ..., 6 with equal weights.

## Check

```python
# E[A] = 3 x 3.5 = 10.5, and the estimate is EXACT, not merely close
assert abs(a.actual_m - 10.5) < 1e-12
assert abs(a.est_m - 10.5) < 1e-9
# support runs 3..18; each extreme has probability (1/6)^3
assert abs(a.density_df.loc[3, 'p_total'] - (1 / 6) ** 3) < 1e-12
assert abs(a.density_df.loc[18, 'p_total'] - (1 / 6) ** 3) < 1e-12
```
}}};

