# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fancy aggregates: one aggregate as the severity of another
# Each pair is inner then outer, and the inner must come first: the outer
# records `sev agg.NAME` at parse time and the underwriter resolves it
# when the outer is built.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

agg NeymanCluster
  4 claims
  dsev [1]
  poisson
  note{One cluster of a Neyman Type A: a Poisson(4) count on a unit severity. The inner half of the construction NeymanInnerOuter assembles.}
  tags{topic:frequency}
  hints{bs=1; log2=8};

agg NeymanInnerOuter
  2.5 claims
  sev agg.NeymanCluster
  poisson
  note{Neyman Type A assembled by hand: a Poisson(2.5) number of NeymanCluster draws, overall mean 10. The same distribution the neymana keyword produces.}
  tags{topic:frequency, check:cross-object}
  hints{bs=1; log2=10}
  doc{{{
## Problem

`neymana` is one of the built in frequency laws: a Poisson number of clusters, each contributing a Poisson number of claims. You want to see what it is actually doing, and you want the same two level shape available when the inner or the outer piece is something the frequency vocabulary does not name.

## Solution

An aggregate can serve as the severity of another aggregate. The inner one, `NeymanCluster`, is a single cluster: a Poisson(4) count on a unit severity. The outer draws a Poisson(2.5) number of clusters and adds them up.

```python
a = build('''<<decl>>''')
qd(a)
```

The keyword states the same law in one line. The overall mean is the product, 2.5 x 4 = 10, and the parameter is the mean cluster size.

```python
builtin = build('agg NeymanBuiltIn 10 claims dsev [1] neymana 4', bs=1, log2=10)
qd(builtin)
```

Compare the two probability mass functions directly.

```python
both = pd.concat([a.density_df.p_total, builtin.density_df.p_total], axis=1)
both.columns = ['inner outer', 'neymana']
both['difference'] = both['inner outer'] - both['neymana']
qd(both.head(12))
```

## Discussion

The inner is referred to by **name**. The outer parses with `agg.NeymanCluster` recorded and the underwriter resolves it at build time, so nothing is spliced textually and the inner is a fully formed object before the outer ever sees it. Its output probability mass function then becomes a discrete severity for the outer, exactly as though it had been transcribed as `dsev [outcomes] [probabilities]` by hand. That is why the inner has to pin `bs` and `log2` in its own `hints`: the severity it hands over must not depend on the settings the outer happens to be built with.

The inner keeps its mass at zero, because a Poisson cluster can be empty and the outer has no layers clause to condition it away. That is load bearing. Neyman A is a Poisson stopped sum of Poissons counting the empty clusters too, and both constructions report the same P(N = 0) = exp(2.5 (exp(-4) - 1)) = 0.0859.

The general fact is that a two level count is a stopped sum, so this construction reaches any of them, not only Neyman A. Pascal is a Poisson stopped sum of negative binomials and is reached the same way, with `negbin` on the inner. The keyword exists because the two level Poisson case is common enough to be worth a closed form probability generating function; the inner outer route is what to reach for when there is no keyword.

## Check

```python
# the two constructions agree to floating point across the whole support
both = pd.concat([a.density_df.p_total, builtin.density_df.p_total], axis=1)
assert (both.iloc[:, 0] - both.iloc[:, 1]).abs().max() < 1e-13
# including the atom at zero, which is what keeping the empty clusters means
assert abs(a.density_df.p_total.iloc[0] - np.exp(2.5 * (np.exp(-4) - 1))) < 1e-9
# and on the moments the keyword computes analytically
assert abs(a.est_m - 10.0) < 1e-9
assert abs(a.est_m - builtin.est_m) < 1e-9
assert abs(a.est_cv - builtin.est_cv) < 1e-9
```
}}};

agg SplitLimitClaimant
  1.25 claims
  100 xs 0
  sev lognorm 50 cv 2
  poisson zt !
  note{Claimants in one US auto 100/300 split limit accident: at least one claimant, 1.25 on average, each capped at the 100 per claimant limit.}
  tags{topic:frequency, topic:severity}
  hints{bs=1/4; log2=13};

agg SplitLimitPolicy
  100 claims
  300 xs 0
  sev agg.SplitLimitClaimant !
  poisson
  note{100 US auto policies written 100/300: the per accident loss of SplitLimitClaimant is the severity, capped at the 300 per accident limit.}
  tags{topic:aggregate, topic:severity, check:reconciliation}
  hints{bs=1/4; log2=17}
  doc{{{
## Problem

A US personal auto policy written 100/300 pays at most 100 to any one claimant and at most 300 for any one accident. Two limits apply at two different levels, and neither is a layer on the other: the 100 sits inside the accident, the 300 sits on the accident total. You want the aggregate loss for a book of such policies.

## Solution

Build the policy from the inside out. The inner aggregate is one accident: a zero truncated Poisson number of claimants, 1.25 of them on average, each subject to the 100 per claimant limit.

```python
inner = build('SplitLimitClaimant')
qd(inner)
```

The outer treats that per accident loss as its severity and applies the 300 as an ordinary occurrence limit.

```python
a = build('''<<decl>>''')
qd(a)
```

## Discussion

The two limits never interact in the program text. The per claimant limit is a layers clause inside the inner, applied claimant by claimant; the per accident limit is a layers clause on the outer, applied to the inner's whole output. Reinsurance clauses would reach the same law and would be much harder to read: prefer no limits, then exposure limits, then aggregate reinsurance, then occurrence reinsurance, in that order.

`poisson zt !` carries two decisions. `zt` truncates the zero, so every draw of the inner has at least one claimant, which is what makes it an accident rather than a policy year. The `!` holds the requested mean at 1.25; without it the 1.25 would be the untruncated Poisson parameter and truncation would lift the mean to 1.75.

The trailing `!` on `sev agg.SplitLimitClaimant` is a different `!`, on the severity side, and it says the outer's `300 xs 0` layer is unconditional. A zero truncated inner has no mass at zero in theory, but any severity with positive density at the origin discretizes a wisp of every claim into the first bucket, about 1.6 in 10,000 at this resolution. Those are small losses, not zero losses. Without the `!` the default conditional reading would drop them and rescale the rest, lifting the severity mean by the same proportion, and the library says so in a warning. Keep the mass.

Both declarations pin `bs` and `log2`, and they pin the **same** `bs`. The inner must pin its resolution because it is materialized into a discrete severity before the outer ever runs, so its output must not depend on the outer's settings. Matching the two is a separate point and it is worth knowing: a layered reference is rebucketed by differencing its survival function, which is exact when the grids agree and loses a few parts in ten thousand when they do not.

The result is worth stating plainly. With 1.25 claimants per accident and each one capped at 100, an accident needs three claimants before the 300 can bind at all, and P(loss to one policy exceeds 300) is about 7 in 100,000. The 300 is worth 0.0019 of expected loss per policy against a per policy mean of 43.92, four thousandths of one percent. In a 100/300 policy the per claimant limit does essentially all the work, and the model shows that rather than asserting it.

## Check

```python
# the frequency ! held the requested mean at 1.25 claimants, against the
# 1.75 that truncating a Poisson(1.25) would otherwise give
assert abs(inner.n - 1.25) < 1e-12
# the outer mean is 100 policies times the per accident loss capped at 300
capped = inner.density_df['lev'].loc[300.0]
assert abs(a.est_m / (100 * capped) - 1) < 1e-9
# the 300 per accident limit binds, but is worth under a hundredth of a percent
saving = inner.est_m - capped
assert 0 < saving < 1e-4 * inner.est_m
```
}}};

